works: {N} and {prospect} find they still have plenty to say after thirty years.
fails: The meeting is awkward, and both of them wish they had left the past alone.
fails: Someone sees them together, and the talk starts.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 6: calls WB (cycle s2) / calls URG (cycle s4)

== a dispute over an inheritance
tier: everyday
stages: mature
age: 40-70
calls: WB
tone: trouble
life: family, money
horizon: months
roles: sibling, dead, a lawyer
earth: after the funeral, you and your siblings cannot agree about your mother's house, her ring and her savings
times: About 1 in 3 families, once, usually after the second parent dies (estimate)
once: yes
share: .33
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: Three weeks after the funeral, {sibling} wants to sell the house, and there is already a lawyer's letter on the kitchen table.
scene W: The will is clear, mostly. {N} believes the fair thing is to follow it to the letter, so that everyone gets what was meant.
scene U: {N} reads the will at the kitchen table with a pencil, and finds a clause that nobody else seems to have noticed.
scene B: {N} looked after {dead} through the last two years, while {sibling} came for the holidays. {N} thinks that should count for something.
scene R: {sibling} is already measuring the dining room for a buyer, and {N} wants to throw something.
scene G: {N} grew up in this house. Selling it to strangers feels like cutting a root.
- W | divide everything exactly as the will says, even the parts you think unfair | identity | v: conformity | chance: 65
