works: The house finds a new rhythm, and {N} likes it more than expected.
works: {child} calls more often from far away than they ever talked at home.
fails: {N} wanders from room to room for months, lost.
fails: The silence in the house becomes the loudest thing in {Ns} life.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: divorce (pairs D5b)

== divorce after twenty years
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 40-80
per_year: 0 0 0 .018 .01 .004
drivers: stress+.5 money-.3 community-.3 era+.3 family-.1 trouble+.15
times: 0 to 2: per married person about 1.8 in 100 adults, 1 in 100 in midlife and 0.4 in 100 elders a year divorce or separate, and about a third of first marriages end that way (D44, data and estimate, Pew 2025); younger couples part through a breakup
likelier: long strain, stress, money trouble, an affair, drinking or addiction, marrying young, less schooling, the children gone, a place where divorce is easy and common
rarer: enough money, a faith or community that forbids it, young children at home, family who back the marriage, money that depends on the marriage, a good marriage
tone: trouble
life: partner, home, money
horizon: years
roles: partner, child, friend, a lawyer
needs: partner
domain: partner1
ends: partner
earth: after twenty-two years, the words are finally said out loud at the kitchen table
scene: After twenty-two years, the words are finally said out loud at the kitchen table: it is over. {partner} has already seen a lawyer.
scene W: {N} made a vow in front of everyone {N} loved. Twenty-two years later it is being taken apart, clause by clause.
scene U: {N} keeps going back over the years, looking for the moment it began to fail, and for what {N} missed.
scene B: Half the house, half the savings, half the pension. {N} sits down with a pad and starts counting what to keep.
scene R: {N} is furious, then heartbroken, then strangely light, all within the same hour.
scene G: Some things grow together for a while and then grow apart. {N} looks at the old wedding photograph and recognises neither face.
- W | insist on a year of counselling before anything is signed | v: tradition, security | chance: 50
- U | read everything on dividing a household fairly, and draw up the plan yourself | v: security, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 60
- B | hire the toughest lawyer in town and fight for every asset | v: power | mark: made an enemy | tags: pay:money-.1 | chance: 48
- R | pack a bag that night and walk out | v: self-direction | mark: left home | chance: 58
- G | let it end gently, as things end, and wish them well | v: universalism (tolerance), benevolence | chance: 70
- BR | empty the joint account before they can, and start a new life | v: power, hedonism | mark: broke your word | closed: law: hiding shared money from the court; backfire: ordered to pay it back, with costs | chance: 50
- RG | sell what you do not need and go to live by the sea | door | v: self-direction, universalism (nature) | mark: moved away | chance: 70
- UG | spend a long season alone, learning who you are outside the marriage | door | v: self-direction | chance: 72
- WB | negotiate a strict settlement in writing, and enforce every line of it | v: security, power | chance: 65
- WU | go to a mediator and agree a fair split of the house and everything else | v: universalism (justice), security | chance: 75
works: The split is done without a war, and {N} and {partner} can still sit in the same room.
works: A year later, {N} wakes up one morning and realises {N} is happy.
fails: The divorce drags on for two years and eats half of everything.
fails: The children take sides, and one of them stops calling.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: your first grandchild (triads from D6a)

== your first grandchild
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 40-85
per_year: 0 0 0 .005 .05 .02
drivers: family+.4 community+.2 era-.3
times: once (the first one): about 5 in 100 a year in midlife, 2 in 100 elders and 0.5 in 100 adults become grandparents for the first time, at about 50 on average (D07, data and estimate, AARP 2019)
likelier: children who became parents young, a religious or rural family, close ties with grown children
rarer: grown children who delay or forgo children, long study, distance or estrangement
tone: joy
life: family
horizon: years
roles: child, partner, parent
needs: children
domain: children1
rite: mature elder
once: yes
earth: your daughter or son phones from the hospital at dawn: the baby is here
scene: The phone rings at dawn. {child} is crying and laughing at once: the baby is here, and {N} is a grandparent.
scene W: {N} drives to the hospital with a bag packed weeks ago: a blanket, a card, and the christening gown {N} once wore.
scene U: {N} holds the baby and studies the tiny face, trying to see whose nose it is, whose eyes.
scene B: Before the thought can be stopped, {N} wonders which side of the family the child will take after, and who will get to see it most.
scene R: {N} has not cried like this since {child} was born. The baby grips {Ns} finger, and that is that.
scene G: Another ring on the tree. Holding the baby, {N} feels {Ns} own parents and grandparents standing somewhere close behind.
- W | offer to look after the baby on one fixed day every week | v: benevolence | chance: 85
- U | read up on how babies are raised now, so your advice is not out of date | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 90
- B | open a savings account in the baby's name, and keep control of it yourself | v: power, benevolence | tags: pay:money-.05 | chance: 90
- R | set off at once and travel through the night to see them | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 88
- G | plant a tree in the garden on the day the baby was born | v: tradition, universalism (nature) | chance: 93
- WUR | organise the naming party, give the speech, and keep the whole room laughing | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 82
- WBR | move in for the first month and run the new household like a sergeant | v: power, benevolence | closed: approval: overbearing; backfire: asked to leave early | chance: 62
- WBG | sell up and move close to the new family, to help with money and time | v: benevolence, tradition | tags: req:money.4; pay:money-.2 | chance: 78
- URG | build the baby a wooden cradle with your own hands, learning as you go | v: benevolence, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- UBG | quietly steer the new parents toward the name and the school you prefer | v: power, tradition | closed: approval: meddling; backfire: the new parents shut the door for a while | chance: 45
works: The baby grows up knowing {Ns} lap as one of the safe places in the world.
works: The new life pulls the whole family closer for a while.
fails: The new parents keep {N} at arm's length, and the visits are rare.
fails: An old quarrel with {child} flares up over how the baby should be raised.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a frightening diagnosis (triads from D6b)

== a frightening diagnosis
tier: life event
at: hospital
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
per_year: 0 0 .0007 .002 .007 .02
drivers: health-.6 money-.3
gap: 3-10
times: 0 to 2: a cancer diagnosis comes to about 0.07 in 100 young adults, 0.2 in 100 adults, 0.7 in 100 in midlife and 2 in 100 elders a year, at a median age of 67 (D64, data and estimate, NCI); heart attacks and strokes (D65) have no event of their own
likelier: age, smoking, heavy drinking, a family history, poverty and a late diagnosis, poor care
rarer: good health habits, screening and good care, money
tone: trouble
life: body
horizon: years
roles: partner, child, friend, a doctor
rite: elder
earth: the scan shows a shadow, and the specialist says the word cancer
scene: The specialist pauses before saying the word, and {N} hears very little after it: a shadow on the scan, more tests, treatment soon.
scene W: {N} is handed a leaflet, a treatment plan and a list of appointments, and holds on to the list as if it were a rope.
scene U: {N} wants the numbers: stages, survival rates, trials, side effects. Fear is easier to face once it has a shape.
scene B: {N} has things to finish and no intention of dying before they are done. Whoever the best surgeon is, {N} wants them.
scene R: {N} walks out into a bright afternoon where everyone else is buying sandwiches, and wants to shout at all of them.
scene G: {N} sits in the car park and thinks of {Ns} parents, of the body that has carried {N} this far, and of what is meant to be.
- W | follow the treatment plan to the letter, and never miss an appointment | habit | v: conformity, security | chance: 65
- U | read every study, and get a second opinion from another hospital | v: security, self-direction | chance: 68
- B | pay to go private and skip the waiting list | v: power, security | tags: req:money.3; pay:money-.15 | chance: 70
- R | tell everyone at once, and refuse to let it stop you living | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 70
- G | accept what comes, and spend the time with the people you love | v: benevolence, tradition (accepting one's lot) | chance: 62
- WUG | build a calm routine around the treatment: walks, good food, early nights | habit | v: security, conformity | chance: 70
- WUB | put your affairs in order: the will, the accounts, who gets what | v: security, power | chance: 75
- WRG | gather the whole family for a big meal, and tell them all together | v: benevolence | chance: 72
- UBR | sign up for a risky experimental trial abroad | door | v: self-direction, security | mark: took a wild risk | tags: pay:money-.1 | tech: modern medicine | chance: 40
- BRG | refuse to slow down, and fight it like an enemy | identity | v: none clear (defiance) | body: heavy | chance: 55
works: The treatment works, and a year later the scans are clear.
works: The illness stays, but {N} learns to live well around it.
fails: The first treatment fails, and the doctors start again.
fails: The treatment takes {Ns} strength for most of a year.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a windfall or an inheritance (cross cycle s1)

== an unexpected legacy
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
per_year: 0 0 .005 .005 .013 .0075
drivers: family+.3 money+.4 prosper+.2 unrest-.3
gap: 5-15
times: 0 to 2: about 1 in 100 young adults and adults, 2.5 in 100 in midlife and 1.5 in 100 elders a year inherit or receive a large gift, and inheritances peak near 60 (D15, data and estimate, Federal Reserve 2018); a big lottery win is under 1 in 100,000. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, 0, .005, .005, .013, .0075 (child to elder): about 4 in 10 people inherit something that matters (estimate from the Fed's Survey of Consumer Finances, where about 1 household in 5 has received one by midlife).
likelier: money in the family, a wealthy, childless relative or godparent, a small family with few heirs, ageing parents or relatives, a family that keeps in touch, a growing economy
rarer: little money anywhere in the family, family strife and disinheritance, unrest that wipes out savings
tone: joy
life: money, family
horizon: years
roles: sibling, partner, child, a solicitor
earth: a solicitor's letter: the aunt you visited twice a year has left you her flat by the sea and her savings
scene: A solicitor's letter: the old aunt {N} visited twice a year has left {N} her flat by the sea and more money than {N} has ever seen in one place.
scene W: {N} feels the weight of it. The old aunt trusted {N} with everything she had built, and that trust has to be honoured.
scene U: {N} sits down with the letter and begins to list what the money could become, given time and good sense.
scene B: {N} reads the figure, then reads it again. For once in {Ns} life there is room to move.
scene R: {N} reads the letter on the doorstep and lets out a whoop that sends the neighbour's cat under the hedge.
scene G: {N} remembers the little flat: shells on the windowsill, the sound of the sea at night. It feels like a gift handed down from the past.
- W | give a tenth to the charity she cared about, and save the rest | v: benevolence, security | chance: 80
- U | leave it untouched for a year while you study what to do with it | v: security | chance: 90
- B | clear every debt, and put the rest where it will grow fastest | v: power, security | tags: win:money+.1 | chance: 82
- R | drop everything and spend a year in her flat by the sea | v: stimulation, hedonism | tags: pay:money-.1 | chance: 83
- G | move into her flat and live quietly by the sea, as she did | door | v: tradition | mark: moved away | chance: 82
- W>U | set up a trust to pay for the family's children through university | binds | v: benevolence | chance: 65
- U>B | time the sale of the flat for the very top of the market | v: achievement, power | tags: win:money+.1 | chance: 55
- B>R | use it to buy yourself years of doing only what you please | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 75
- R>G | share it out on the spot among the whole family | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- G>W | keep the flat as a holiday home for the whole family to share | binds | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 68
works: The money brings {N} a few years of rare freedom, and {N} uses them well.
works: Years later, {N} still says a quiet word of thanks every time {N} passes the sea.
fails: The money slips away far faster than {N} thought possible.
fails: A cousin contests the will, and the joy turns into a long legal fight.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a chance to change everything (the four-color set)

== a chance to change everything
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 42-70
per_year: 0 0 0 .03 .02 .01
drivers: money+.4 ties+.3 community+.2 stress-.2 unrest-.3 fortune+.1
gap: 5-10
times: 0 to 2: about 3 in 100 adults, 2 in 100 in midlife and 1 in 100 elders a year find a calling or a new direction (D25, estimate)
likelier: savings and a paid-off home, a buyout or a redundancy offer, grown children, schooling, friends or a mentor who encourage it, a faith or community behind the change
rarer: low money and debts, dependants, family pressure to stay put, stress, unrest, a family trade or a place that will not let go
tone: mixed
life: work, home, meaning
horizon: years
roles: partner, friend, child, boss
rite: mature elder
earth: a buyout at work, a sale or a small legacy would cover two lean years, and for the first time in decades the next step is wide open
scene: The house is nearly paid, the heaviest duties of the middle years are lifting, and a buyout offer would cover two lean years. For the first time in thirty years, the next step is wide open.
scene W: {N} has always known what was expected next. Now nobody expects anything, and {N} finds that harder than any rule.
scene U: {N} draws two columns on the back of an envelope: the life {N} has, and the one {N} could build from here.
scene B: The offer is generous. With the savings, it makes a stake big enough to bet on something of {Ns} own.
scene R: {N} reads the offer and feels the old wildness rise: the boat, the road, the thing never tried.
scene G: Halfway up the hill of a life, {N} stops to look back down the path, and up at the rest of the slope.
- W | turn it down, and keep faith with the people who depend on you | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: turned down a chance | act: turn it down, and keep faith with the people who depend on them | chance: 65
- U | take a year to research and test three possible new lives before choosing | door | v: self-direction | chance: 72
- B | take the money and buy a business that already turns a profit | binds | v: power, achievement | tags: win:money+.1 | chance: 74
- R | take the money, and go back to the thing you loved and gave up at twenty-five | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 68
- G | take the money, move back to where you grew up, and slow down | identity | v: tradition | mark: moved away | chance: 72
- WUBG | take the money, spend three years retraining for work your home town needs, and stay | binds | v: achievement, benevolence | chance: 65
- WUBR | take the money, write a proper business plan, and build the invention you sketched years ago | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 40
- WURG | take the money, and give two years to rebuilding schools where the need is greatest | door | v: universalism, benevolence | mark: moved away | chance: 75
- WBRG | buy a run-down farm with the money, and fight to make the land pay | binds | v: achievement, tradition | body: heavy | grants: plot of land | chance: 40
- UBRG | sell up, cut every tie, and sail a small boat around the world | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: left home | chance: 50
works: Two years on, {N} is sure it was the right choice.
works: The path {N} chose turns out harder than expected, and better.
fails: The money runs out before {Ns} plans find their feet.
fails: {N} spends the next year looking back at the other road.
